      "text": "We first find the prime factorization of each number from 1 to 15:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n1 &= 1^1, \\\\\n2 &= 2^1, \\\\\n3 &= 3^1, \\\\\n4 &= 2^2, \\\\\n5 &= 5^1, \\\\\n6 &= 2^1 \\cdot 3^1, \\\\\n7 &= 7^1, \\\\\n8 &= 2^3, \\\\\n9 &= 3^2, \\\\\n10 &= 2^1 \\cdot 5^1, \\\\\n11 &= 11^1, \\\\\n12 &= 2^2 \\cdot 3^1, \\\\\n13 &= 13^1, \\\\\n14 &= 2^1 \\cdot 7^1, \\\\\n15 &= 3^1 \\cdot 5^1.\n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, we count the number of divisors for each number:\n\\begin{align*}\n1 &= 1^1 &\\quad\\text{(1 divisor)} \\\\\n2 &= 2^1 &\\quad\\text{(2 divisors)} \\\\\n3 &= 3^1 &\\quad\\text{(2 divisors)} \\\\\n4 &= 2^2 &\\quad\\text{(3 divisors)} \\\\\n5 &= 5^1 &\\quad\\text{(2 divisors)} \\\\\n6 &= 2^1 \\cdot 3^1 &\\quad\\text{(4 divisors)} \\\\\n7 &= 7^1 &\\quad\\text{(2 divisors)} \\\\\n8 &= 2^3 &\\quad\\text{(4 divisors)} \\\\\n9 &= 3^2 &\\quad\\text{(3 divisors)} \\\\\n10 &= 2^1 \\cdot 5^1 &\\quad\\text{(4 divisors)} \\\\\n11 &= 11^1 &\\quad\\text{(2 divisors)} \\\\\n12 &= 2^2 \\cdot 3^1 &\\quad\\text{(6 divisors)} \\\\\n13 &= 13^1 &\\quad\\text{(2 divisors)} \\\\\n14 &= 2^1 \\cdot 7^1 &\\quad\\text{(3 divisors)} \\\\\n15 &= 3^1 \\cdot 5^1 &\\quad\\text{(4 divisors)}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nThe number 12 has the greatest number of divisors, which is 6.\n\nThus, the numbers 1 through 15 has the greatest number of divisors is $\\boxed{12}$.",
