      "text": "To determine which number between 1 and 15 has the greatest number of divisors, we need to calculate the number of divisors for each of these numbers. The number of divisors of a number can be determined by its prime factorization. If a number \\( n \\) has a prime factorization of \\( n = p_1^{e_1} p_2^{e_2} \\cdots p_k^{e_k} \\), then the number of divisors \\( d(n) \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nd(n) = (e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1)\n\\]\n\nWe will calculate the number of divisors for each number from 1 to 15.\n\n1. **Number 1**: \\( 1 = 1^1 \\)\n   \\[\n   d(1) = 1 + 1 = 2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Number 2**: \\( 2 = 2^1 \\)\n   \\[\n   d(2) = 1 + 1 = 2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Number 3**: \\( 3 = 3^1 \\)\n   \\[\n   d(3) = 1 + 1 = 2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Number 4**: \\( 4 = 2^2 \\)\n   \\[\n   d(4) = 2 + 1 = 3\n   \\]\n\n5. **Number 5**: \\( 5 = 5^1 \\)\n   \\[\n   d(5) = 1 + 1 = 2\n   \\]\n\n6. **Number 6**: \\( 6 = 2 \\times 3 \\)\n   \\[\n   d(6) = (1 + 1)(1 + 1) = 2 \\times 2 = 4\n   \\]\n\n7. **Number 7**: \\( 7 = 7^1 \\)\n   \\[\n   d(7) = 1 + 1 = 2\n   \\]\n\n8. **Number 8**: \\( 8 = 2^3 \\)\n   \\[\n   d(8) = 3 + 1 = 4\n   \\]\n\n9. **Number 9**: \\( 9 = 3^2 \\)\n   \\[\n   d(9) = 2 + 1 = 3\n   \\]\n\n10. **Number 10**: \\( 10 = 2 \\times 5 \\)\n    \\[\n    d(10) = (1 + 1)(1 + 1) = 2 \\times 2 = 4\n    \\]\n\n11. **Number 11**: \\( 11 = 11^1 \\)\n    \\[\n    d(11) = 1 + 1 = 2\n    \\]\n\n12. **Number 12**: \\( 12 = 2^2 \\times 3 \\)\n    \\[\n    d(12) = (2 + 1)(1 + 1) = 3 \\times 2 = 6\n    \\]\n\n13. **Number 13**: \\( 13 = 13^1 \\)\n    \\[\n    d(13) = 1 + 1 = 2\n    \\]\n\n14. **Number 14**: \\( 14 = 2 \\times 7 \\)\n    \\[\n    d(14) = (1 + 1)(1 + 1) = 2 \\times 2 = 4\n    \\]\n\n15. **Number 15**: \\( 15 = 3 \\times 5 \\)\n    \\[\n    d(15) = (1 + 1)(1 + 1) = 2 \\times 2 = 4\n    \\]\n\nFrom the calculations, we see that the number with the greatest number of divisors between 1 and 15 is 12, with \\( d(12) = 6 \\).\n\nThus, the number with the greatest number of divisors is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).",
