      "text": "To determine which number between 1 and 15 has the greatest number of divisors, we need to calculate the number of divisors for each number and then compare the results. The number of divisors of a number depends on its prime factorization.\n\nThe number of divisors \\(d(n)\\) of a number \\(n\\) with the prime factorization \\(n = p_1^{e_1} p_2^{e_2} \\cdots p_k^{e_k}\\) is given by:\n\\[ d(n) = (e_1 + 1)(e_2 + 1) \\cdots (e_k + 1) \\]\n\nLet's calculate the number of divisors for each number from 1 to 15.\n\n1. \\(1 = 1^1\\) has \\(d(1) = 2\\) divisors.\n2. \\(2 = 2^1\\) has \\(d(2) = 2\\) divisors.\n3. \\(3 = 3^1\\) has \\(d(3) = 2\\) divisors.\n4. \\(4 = 2^2\\) has \\(d(4) = (2+1) = 3\\) divisors.\n5. \\(5 = 5^1\\) has \\(d(5) = 2\\) divisors.\n6. \\(6 = 2^1 \\times 3^1\\) has \\(d(6) = (1+1)(1+1) = 4\\) divisors.\n7. \\(7 = 7^1\\) has \\(d(7) = 2\\) divisors.\n8. \\(8 = 2^3\\) has \\(d(8) = (3+1) = 4\\) divisors.\n9. \\(9 = 3^2\\) has \\(d(9) = (2+1) = 3\\) divisors.\n10. \\(10 = 2^1 \\times 5^1\\) has \\(d(10) = (1+1)(1+1) = 4\\) divisors.\n11. \\(11 = 11^1\\) has \\(d(11) = 2\\) divisors.\n12. \\(12 = 2^2 \\times 3^1\\) has \\(d(12) = (2+1)(1+1) = 6\\) divisors.\n13. \\(13 = 13^1\\) has \\(d(13) = 2\\) divisors.\n14. \\(14 = 2^1 \\times 7^1\\) has \\(d(14) = (1+1)(1+1) = 4\\) divisors.\n15. \\(15 = 3^1 \\times 5^1\\) has \\(d(15) = (1+1)(1+1) = 4\\) divisors.\n\nNow, let's list the number of divisors for each number:\n- \\(1\\): 2\n- \\(2\\): 2\n- \\(3\\): 2\n- \\(4\\): 3\n- \\(5\\): 2\n- \\(6\\): 4\n- \\(7\\): 2\n- \\(8\\): 4\n- \\(9\\): 3\n- \\(10\\): 4\n- \\(11\\): 2\n- \\(12\\): 6\n- \\(13\\): 2\n- \\(14\\): 4\n- \\(15\\): 4\n\nThe number with the greatest number of divisors is 12, with \\(d(12) = 6\\).\n\nThus, the number between 1 and 15 that has the greatest number of divisors is \\(\\boxed{12}\\).",
